{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b1433e221b","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-354","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Fenwick is in eastern Poland. It was first surveyed in 1948.</li><li>Its surface area is 548 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Eastwick is in western Scotland. It was first surveyed in 1973.</li><li>Its surface area is 498 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are reservoirs created by dams.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between Lake Fenwick and Lake Eastwick.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Lake Fenwick is in eastern Poland, and Lake Eastwick is in western Scotland.","Both Lake Fenwick and Lake Eastwick are reservoirs created by dams.","Lake Fenwick was first surveyed in 1948, while Lake Eastwick was first surveyed in 1973.","The surface area of Lake Fenwick (548 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Eastwick (498 square kilometers)."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.95e3358847","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-354","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Fenwick is in eastern Poland. It was first surveyed in 1948.</li><li>Its surface area is 548 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Eastwick is in western Scotland. It was first surveyed in 1973.</li><li>Its surface area is 498 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are reservoirs created by dams.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the surface area of the two lakes differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The surface area of Lake Fenwick (548 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Eastwick (498 square kilometers).","Lake Fenwick was first surveyed in 1948, while Lake Eastwick was first surveyed in 1973.","Both Lake Fenwick and Lake Eastwick are reservoirs created by dams.","Lake Fenwick, which is in eastern Poland, is one of several reservoirs created by dams."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the surface area of both lakes. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one lake."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b078f80627","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-355","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Fenwick is in central Spain. It was first surveyed in 1982.</li><li>Its surface area is 822 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Merrow is in northern Japan. It was first surveyed in 1977.</li><li>Its surface area is 262 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are reservoirs created by dams.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between Lake Fenwick and Lake Merrow.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The surface area of Lake Fenwick (822 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Merrow (262 square kilometers).","Lake Fenwick is in central Spain, and Lake Merrow is in northern Japan.","Both Lake Fenwick and Lake Merrow are reservoirs created by dams.","Lake Fenwick was first surveyed in 1982, while Lake Merrow was first surveyed in 1977."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.2770d9c59d","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-356","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Yarrow Museum is in central Spain. It opened in 1951.</li><li>Its annual attendance is 896,590 visitors.</li><li>The Brookhaven Museum is in northern Chile. It opened in 1936.</li><li>Its annual attendance is 293,186 visitors.</li><li>Both are museums of natural history.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two museums opened.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The annual attendance of the Yarrow Museum (896,590 visitors) is greater than that of the Brookhaven Museum (293,186 visitors).","The Yarrow Museum opened in 1951, while the Brookhaven Museum opened in 1936.","Both the Yarrow Museum and the Brookhaven Museum are museums of natural history.","The Yarrow Museum is in central Spain, and the Brookhaven Museum is in northern Chile."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.6ca72cd85b","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-363","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Mount Halden is in eastern Australia. It was first climbed in 2011.</li><li>Its height is 3,565 meters.</li><li>Mount Merrow is in northern Japan. It was first climbed in 1974.</li><li>Its height is 2,013 meters.</li><li>Both are dormant volcanoes.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two mountains were first climbed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Mount Halden was first climbed in 2011, while Mount Merrow was first climbed in 1974.","The height of Mount Halden (3,565 meters) is greater than that of Mount Merrow (2,013 meters).","Both Mount Halden and Mount Merrow are dormant volcanoes.","Mount Halden is in eastern Australia, and Mount Merrow is in northern Japan."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.80c317544c","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-371","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Orla National Park is in central Canada. It was established in 1990.</li><li>Its area is 1,115 square kilometers.</li><li>Umber National Park is in western Kenya. It was established in 1930.</li><li>Its area is 206 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are parks that protect coastal wetlands.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between Orla National Park and Umber National Park.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Orla National Park was established in 1990, while Umber National Park was established in 1930.","The area of Orla National Park (1,115 square kilometers) is greater than that of Umber National Park (206 square kilometers).","Both Orla National Park and Umber National Park are parks that protect coastal wetlands.","Orla National Park is in central Canada, and Umber National Park is in western Kenya."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.4d6855feaa","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-375","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Thornbury is in central Canada. It was first surveyed in 1936.</li><li>Its surface area is 636 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Wexford is in northern India. It was first surveyed in 1985.</li><li>Its surface area is 500 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are freshwater lakes formed by glaciers.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the surface area of the two lakes differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The surface area of Lake Thornbury (636 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Wexford (500 square kilometers).","Lake Thornbury was first surveyed in 1936, while Lake Wexford was first surveyed in 1985.","Both Lake Thornbury and Lake Wexford are freshwater lakes formed by glaciers.","Lake Thornbury, which is in central Canada, is one of several freshwater lakes formed by glaciers."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the surface area of both lakes. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one lake."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.472a0a4377","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-377","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Glenrock Observatory is in northern Chile. It began operating in 1909.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 11 meters.</li><li>The Eastwick Observatory is in southern New Zealand. It began operating in 1963.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 2 meters.</li><li>Both are observatories located on remote mountaintops.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two observatorys began operating.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both the Glenrock Observatory and the Eastwick Observatory are observatories located on remote mountaintops.","The main telescope's mirror diameter of the Glenrock Observatory (11 meters) is greater than that of the Eastwick Observatory (2 meters).","The Glenrock Observatory is in northern Chile, and the Eastwick Observatory is in southern New Zealand.","The Glenrock Observatory began operating in 1909, while the Eastwick Observatory began operating in 1963."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.9f24a2948b","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-385","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Wexford Tower is in central Canada. It was completed in 1928.</li><li>Its height is 348 meters.</li><li>The Northam Tower is in northern India. It was completed in 1925.</li><li>Its height is 195 meters.</li><li>Both are towers built mainly of steel.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two towers were completed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Wexford Tower is in central Canada, and the Northam Tower is in northern India.","The height of the Wexford Tower (348 meters) is greater than that of the Northam Tower (195 meters).","Both the Wexford Tower and the Northam Tower are towers built mainly of steel.","The Wexford Tower was completed in 1928, while the Northam Tower was completed in 1925."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.7e0276220a","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-386","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Pembrook Bridge is in western Kenya. It opened in 1894.</li><li>Its main span is 2,037 meters.</li><li>The Glenrock Bridge is in northern India. It opened in 1961.</li><li>Its main span is 1,342 meters.</li><li>Both are cable-stayed bridges.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two bridges opened.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Pembrook Bridge opened in 1894, while the Glenrock Bridge opened in 1961.","The main span of the Pembrook Bridge (2,037 meters) is greater than that of the Glenrock Bridge (1,342 meters).","Both the Pembrook Bridge and the Glenrock Bridge are cable-stayed bridges.","The Pembrook Bridge is in western Kenya, and the Glenrock Bridge is in northern India."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.a8a75915fe","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-397","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Umber Museum is in central Spain. It opened in 1929.</li><li>Its annual attendance is 319,340 visitors.</li><li>The Halden Museum is in eastern Australia. It opened in 1918.</li><li>Its annual attendance is 309,707 visitors.</li><li>Both are museums of natural history.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the Umber Museum and the Halden Museum.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Umber Museum is in central Spain, and the Halden Museum is in eastern Australia.","The Umber Museum opened in 1929, while the Halden Museum opened in 1918.","Both the Umber Museum and the Halden Museum are museums of natural history.","The annual attendance of the Umber Museum (319,340 visitors) is greater than that of the Halden Museum (309,707 visitors)."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.c58de60ca1","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-397","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Umber Museum is in central Spain. It opened in 1929.</li><li>Its annual attendance is 319,340 visitors.</li><li>The Halden Museum is in eastern Australia. It opened in 1918.</li><li>Its annual attendance is 309,707 visitors.</li><li>Both are museums of natural history.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two museums opened.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Umber Museum opened in 1929, while the Halden Museum opened in 1918.","The annual attendance of the Umber Museum (319,340 visitors) is greater than that of the Halden Museum (309,707 visitors).","Both the Umber Museum and the Halden Museum are museums of natural history.","The Umber Museum is in central Spain, and the Halden Museum is in eastern Australia."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.607993c9b4","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-4","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Nadia Kowalski is a marine biologist.</li><li>Kowalski wanted to find out whether noise from passing ships affects how often humpback whales sing.</li><li>Kowalski recorded whale songs in a busy shipping lane and in a quiet bay for two months.</li><li>Kowalski found that whales in the shipping lane sang about 40 percent less often.</li><li>Kowalski concluded that ship noise may interfere with whale communication.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Kowalski recorded whale songs in a busy shipping lane and in a quiet bay for two months.","Kowalski found that whales in the shipping lane sang about 40 percent less often.","Marine biologist Nadia Kowalski set out to learn whether noise from passing ships affects how often humpback whales sing.","Kowalski concluded that ship noise may interfere with whale communication."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.3c77c3dd8b","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-8","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lucía Sato is an ecologist.</li><li>Sato wanted to find out whether city lights change when songbirds begin singing in the morning.</li><li>Sato compared the first songs of robins in brightly lit city parks with those in dark rural woodlands.</li><li>Sato found that robins in lit parks began singing nearly an hour earlier.</li><li>Sato concluded that artificial light may shift birds' daily rhythms.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Ecologist Lucía Sato set out to learn whether city lights change when songbirds begin singing in the morning.","To investigate the question, Sato compared the first songs of robins in brightly lit city parks with those in dark rural woodlands.","Sato found that robins in lit parks began singing nearly an hour earlier.","Sato concluded that artificial light may shift birds' daily rhythms."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.552d113a11","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-14","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Elias Novak is a psychologist.</li><li>Novak wanted to find out whether taking short breaks improves students' focus during long study sessions.</li><li>Novak had one group of students take a five-minute break every half hour while another group studied without breaks.</li><li>Novak found that students who took breaks answered more practice questions correctly.</li><li>Novak concluded that brief breaks may help people stay focused.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Novak concluded that brief breaks may help people stay focused.","Novak found that students who took breaks answered more practice questions correctly.","To investigate the question, Novak had one group of students take a five-minute break every half hour while another group studied without breaks.","Psychologist Elias Novak set out to learn whether taking short breaks improves students' focus during long study sessions."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.cb222dd02b","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-35","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Dmitri Solberg is an entomologist.</li><li>Solberg wanted to find out whether bees prefer flowers of a particular color.</li><li>Solberg set out identical feeders colored blue, yellow, and white in a meadow.</li><li>Solberg found that bees visited the blue feeders most often.</li><li>Solberg concluded that color may play a role in how bees choose flowers.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Solberg found that bees visited the blue feeders most often.","To investigate the question, Solberg set out identical feeders colored blue, yellow, and white in a meadow.","Entomologist Dmitri Solberg set out to learn whether bees prefer flowers of a particular color.","Solberg concluded that color may play a role in how bees choose flowers."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.910de4a414","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-46","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kenji Okafor is a geologist.</li><li>Okafor wanted to find out whether a hillside's plant cover affects how much soil washes away in heavy rain.</li><li>Okafor measured soil runoff on bare slopes and on slopes covered with native grasses.</li><li>Okafor found that bare slopes lost several times more soil.</li><li>Okafor concluded that plant cover may protect hillsides from erosion.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Okafor found that bare slopes lost several times more soil.","To investigate the question, Okafor measured soil runoff on bare slopes and on slopes covered with native grasses.","Geologist Kenji Okafor set out to learn whether a hillside's plant cover affects how much soil washes away in heavy rain.","Okafor concluded that plant cover may protect hillsides from erosion."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.dbbc962652","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-53","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Felipe Kowalski is a nutrition researcher.</li><li>Kowalski wanted to find out whether eating breakfast affects how much food people eat at lunch.</li><li>Kowalski asked volunteers to eat breakfast on some days and skip it on others, then measured their lunch portions.</li><li>Kowalski found that lunch portions were about the same on both kinds of days.</li><li>Kowalski concluded that skipping breakfast may not lead people to eat more later.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Kowalski concluded that skipping breakfast may not lead people to eat more later.","To investigate the question, Kowalski asked volunteers to eat breakfast on some days and skip it on others, then measured their lunch portions.","Nutrition researcher Felipe Kowalski set out to learn whether eating breakfast affects how much food people eat at lunch.","Kowalski found that lunch portions were about the same on both kinds of days."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.454abe162a","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-54","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kenji Haddad is a zoologist.</li><li>Haddad wanted to find out whether zoo elephants that are given puzzle feeders are more active.</li><li>Haddad gave puzzle feeders to some elephants and standard food trays to others, then tracked their movement.</li><li>Haddad found that elephants with puzzle feeders walked noticeably more each day.</li><li>Haddad concluded that puzzle feeders may encourage healthy activity.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Haddad found that elephants with puzzle feeders walked noticeably more each day.","To investigate the question, Haddad gave puzzle feeders to some elephants and standard food trays to others, then tracked their movement.","Zoologist Kenji Haddad set out to learn whether zoo elephants that are given puzzle feeders are more active.","Haddad concluded that puzzle feeders may encourage healthy activity."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.60e99449e4","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-62","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Beatriz Halvorsen is an astronomer.</li><li>Halvorsen wanted to find out whether a distant star's brightness changes on a regular schedule.</li><li>Halvorsen measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months.</li><li>Halvorsen found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days.</li><li>Halvorsen concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Astronomer Beatriz Halvorsen set out to learn whether a distant star's brightness changes on a regular schedule.","Halvorsen concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star.","To investigate the question, Halvorsen measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months.","Halvorsen found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.17a182837d","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-65","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Leila Castellano is an astronomer.</li><li>Castellano wanted to find out whether a distant star's brightness changes on a regular schedule.</li><li>Castellano measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months.</li><li>Castellano found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days.</li><li>Castellano concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Castellano concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star.","Astronomer Leila Castellano set out to learn whether a distant star's brightness changes on a regular schedule.","Castellano found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days.","To investigate the question, Castellano measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.29c12d975d","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-75","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Tomás Varga is an economist.</li><li>Varga wanted to find out whether a four-day workweek changes employee productivity.</li><li>Varga tracked output at companies before and after they adopted a four-day week.</li><li>Varga found that output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly.</li><li>Varga concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Economist Tomás Varga set out to learn whether a four-day workweek changes employee productivity.","To investigate the question, Varga tracked output at companies before and after they adopted a four-day week.","Varga concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.","Varga found that output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.19e7ddc19c","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-91","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Mei Halvorsen is a hydrologist.</li><li>Halvorsen wanted to find out whether wetlands reduce flooding downstream.</li><li>Halvorsen compared river levels below restored wetlands with levels below drained farmland after storms.</li><li>Halvorsen found that river levels rose more slowly below the wetlands.</li><li>Halvorsen concluded that wetlands may help protect nearby communities from floods.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Halvorsen concluded that wetlands may help protect nearby communities from floods.","To investigate the question, Halvorsen compared river levels below restored wetlands with levels below drained farmland after storms.","Hydrologist Mei Halvorsen set out to learn whether wetlands reduce flooding downstream.","Halvorsen found that river levels rose more slowly below the wetlands."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.8ea8f1b3f4","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-92","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ingrid Castellano is a hydrologist.</li><li>Castellano wanted to find out whether wetlands reduce flooding downstream.</li><li>Castellano compared river levels below restored wetlands with levels below drained farmland after storms.</li><li>Castellano found that river levels rose more slowly below the wetlands.</li><li>Castellano concluded that wetlands may help protect nearby communities from floods.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Castellano concluded that wetlands may help protect nearby communities from floods.","To investigate the question, Castellano compared river levels below restored wetlands with levels below drained farmland after storms.","Hydrologist Ingrid Castellano set out to learn whether wetlands reduce flooding downstream.","Castellano found that river levels rose more slowly below the wetlands."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.1eb977ab62","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-99","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Diego Castellano is an education researcher.</li><li>Castellano wanted to find out whether reading aloud to young children improves their vocabulary.</li><li>Castellano had parents in one group read aloud daily while another group continued their usual routines.</li><li>Castellano found that children in the read-aloud group learned more new words.</li><li>Castellano concluded that regular read-aloud time may support vocabulary growth.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Castellano found that children in the read-aloud group learned more new words.","Education researcher Diego Castellano set out to learn whether reading aloud to young children improves their vocabulary.","To investigate the question, Castellano had parents in one group read aloud daily while another group continued their usual routines.","Castellano concluded that regular read-aloud time may support vocabulary growth."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.5fdeac4c34","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-106","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Yara Brennan is a chemist.</li><li>Brennan wanted to find out whether a new plant-based plastic breaks down in seawater.</li><li>Brennan placed samples of the plastic in tanks of seawater for six months.</li><li>Brennan found that most samples broke apart within four months.</li><li>Brennan concluded that the plastic may be less harmful to oceans than conventional plastic.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Brennan placed samples of the plastic in tanks of seawater for six months.","Chemist Yara Brennan set out to learn whether a new plant-based plastic breaks down in seawater.","Brennan found that most samples broke apart within four months.","Brennan concluded that the plastic may be less harmful to oceans than conventional plastic."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.4ea35b1783","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-108","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Leila Varga is a primatologist.</li><li>Varga wanted to find out whether young chimpanzees learn to use tools by watching adults.</li><li>Varga observed young chimpanzees in groups where adults cracked nuts with stones and in groups where adults did not.</li><li>Varga found that young chimpanzees in the nut-cracking groups began using stones much earlier.</li><li>Varga concluded that young chimpanzees may learn tool use by observation.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Varga found that young chimpanzees in the nut-cracking groups began using stones much earlier.","Primatologist Leila Varga set out to learn whether young chimpanzees learn to use tools by watching adults.","Varga concluded that young chimpanzees may learn tool use by observation.","To investigate the question, Varga observed young chimpanzees in groups where adults cracked nuts with stones and in groups where adults did not."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.e2762b851f","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-109","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lucía Petrov is a primatologist.</li><li>Petrov wanted to find out whether young chimpanzees learn to use tools by watching adults.</li><li>Petrov observed young chimpanzees in groups where adults cracked nuts with stones and in groups where adults did not.</li><li>Petrov found that young chimpanzees in the nut-cracking groups began using stones much earlier.</li><li>Petrov concluded that young chimpanzees may learn tool use by observation.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Petrov observed young chimpanzees in groups where adults cracked nuts with stones and in groups where adults did not.","Petrov found that young chimpanzees in the nut-cracking groups began using stones much earlier.","Primatologist Lucía Petrov set out to learn whether young chimpanzees learn to use tools by watching adults.","Petrov concluded that young chimpanzees may learn tool use by observation."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.dfe559db7b","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-121","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Tomás Ferreira is an ornithologist.</li><li>Ferreira wanted to find out whether birds that migrate farther have longer wings.</li><li>Ferreira measured the wings of several related species with different migration distances.</li><li>Ferreira found that species that migrated farther tended to have longer, narrower wings.</li><li>Ferreira concluded that wing shape may be linked to migration distance.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Ferreira concluded that wing shape may be linked to migration distance.","To investigate the question, Ferreira measured the wings of several related species with different migration distances.","Ornithologist Tomás Ferreira set out to learn whether birds that migrate farther have longer wings.","Ferreira found that species that migrated farther tended to have longer, narrower wings."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.4823daff18","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-126","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Yara Delacroix is a public health researcher.</li><li>Delacroix wanted to find out whether adding bike lanes increases how often residents cycle.</li><li>Delacroix counted cyclists on streets before and after new bike lanes were added.</li><li>Delacroix found that cycling on those streets nearly doubled within a year.</li><li>Delacroix concluded that bike lanes may encourage more people to ride.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Public health researcher Yara Delacroix set out to learn whether adding bike lanes increases how often residents cycle.","Delacroix found that cycling on those streets nearly doubled within a year.","To investigate the question, Delacroix counted cyclists on streets before and after new bike lanes were added.","Delacroix concluded that bike lanes may encourage more people to ride."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.ccd01e30b4","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-134","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ravi Duarte is an archaeologist.</li><li>Duarte wanted to find out whether a coastal settlement traded with inland villages.</li><li>Duarte analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement.</li><li>Duarte found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland.</li><li>Duarte concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Duarte analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement.","Archaeologist Ravi Duarte set out to learn whether a coastal settlement traded with inland villages.","Duarte concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.","Duarte found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b5d5a5158c","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-137","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Mateo Quintero is an archaeologist.</li><li>Quintero wanted to find out whether a coastal settlement traded with inland villages.</li><li>Quintero analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement.</li><li>Quintero found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland.</li><li>Quintero concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Quintero concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.","To investigate the question, Quintero analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement.","Quintero found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland.","Archaeologist Mateo Quintero set out to learn whether a coastal settlement traded with inland villages."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.237a1c9b0e","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-138","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kwame Okafor is a neuroscientist.</li><li>Okafor wanted to find out whether learning to juggle changes brain structure.</li><li>Okafor scanned volunteers' brains before and after three months of juggling practice.</li><li>Okafor found that regions linked to tracking movement grew slightly larger.</li><li>Okafor concluded that practicing a new skill may reshape parts of the brain.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Okafor found that regions linked to tracking movement grew slightly larger.","To investigate the question, Okafor scanned volunteers' brains before and after three months of juggling practice.","Neuroscientist Kwame Okafor set out to learn whether learning to juggle changes brain structure.","Okafor concluded that practicing a new skill may reshape parts of the brain."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.ee1c9a3799","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-150","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Chen Varga is a climate scientist.</li><li>Varga wanted to find out whether light-colored roofs reduce indoor temperatures.</li><li>Varga compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.</li><li>Varga found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler.</li><li>Varga concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Varga found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler.","Climate scientist Chen Varga set out to learn whether light-colored roofs reduce indoor temperatures.","To investigate the question, Varga compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.","Varga concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.22f078abea","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-164","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lucía Kowalski is a veterinary scientist.</li><li>Kowalski wanted to find out whether dogs recognize their owners' voices.</li><li>Kowalski played recordings of owners' voices and strangers' voices to dogs.</li><li>Kowalski found that dogs turned toward their owners' voices far more often.</li><li>Kowalski concluded that dogs may be able to tell their owners' voices apart from others.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Kowalski played recordings of owners' voices and strangers' voices to dogs.","Kowalski found that dogs turned toward their owners' voices far more often.","Veterinary scientist Lucía Kowalski set out to learn whether dogs recognize their owners' voices.","Kowalski concluded that dogs may be able to tell their owners' voices apart from others."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.bc769415ee","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-165","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lucía Ferreira is a veterinary scientist.</li><li>Ferreira wanted to find out whether dogs recognize their owners' voices.</li><li>Ferreira played recordings of owners' voices and strangers' voices to dogs.</li><li>Ferreira found that dogs turned toward their owners' voices far more often.</li><li>Ferreira concluded that dogs may be able to tell their owners' voices apart from others.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Ferreira played recordings of owners' voices and strangers' voices to dogs.","Veterinary scientist Lucía Ferreira set out to learn whether dogs recognize their owners' voices.","Ferreira found that dogs turned toward their owners' voices far more often.","Ferreira concluded that dogs may be able to tell their owners' voices apart from others."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.0eba6a9a16","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-170","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ravi Adeyemi is a forest ecologist.</li><li>Adeyemi wanted to find out whether fungi help trees share nutrients.</li><li>Adeyemi added a traceable form of carbon to some trees and checked nearby trees for it.</li><li>Adeyemi found that the carbon appeared in neighboring trees connected by fungal networks.</li><li>Adeyemi concluded that fungal networks may move nutrients between trees.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Adeyemi concluded that fungal networks may move nutrients between trees.","To investigate the question, Adeyemi added a traceable form of carbon to some trees and checked nearby trees for it.","Adeyemi found that the carbon appeared in neighboring trees connected by fungal networks.","Forest ecologist Ravi Adeyemi set out to learn whether fungi help trees share nutrients."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.1ad8d4c9f2","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-174","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Sofia Okafor is a music researcher.</li><li>Okafor wanted to find out whether learning an instrument improves children's listening skills.</li><li>Okafor tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons.</li><li>Okafor found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not.</li><li>Okafor concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Okafor concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills.","Music researcher Sofia Okafor set out to learn whether learning an instrument improves children's listening skills.","To investigate the question, Okafor tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons.","Okafor found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."}
